PullRequest.Body:
Fixes #200206 by warning in Editor Language Status when the existing 500-diagnostic highlighting limit is exceeded. The limit is additionally increased to 5000 - please see below for details.

## Important Note

The marker decoration limit and the diagnostic limit apply at different stages. The Extension Host currently allows up to 1,000 diagnostics per file, per diagnostic collection, while the marker decoration limit applies to the combined markers from all collections and internal sources.

This means increasing the decoration limit to 5,000 could help files receiving diagnostics from several providers, but it would not materially affect the common case of a single provider such as TypeScript, since that collection is capped at 1,000 diagnostics plus the truncation message.

If the goal is to display substantially more diagnostics from an individual provider, perhaps we should also consider increasing _maxDiagnosticsPerFile. 

### Testing example

To make more than 5000 diagnostics appear, you can create an extension with the following code:

```
> package.json 

    "contributes": {
        "commands": [
            {
                "command": "markerPerf.publish",
                "title": "Marker Perf: Publish 6000 Diagnostics"
            },
            {
                "command": "markerPerf.clear",
                "title": "Marker Perf: Clear Diagnostics"
            }
        ]
    }
```

And:

```
> extension.ts

const vscode = require('vscode');

function activate(context) {
    const collections = Array.from(
        { length: 6 },
        (_, index) => vscode.languages.createDiagnosticCollection(`marker-perf-${index}`)
    );

    context.subscriptions.push(
        ...collections,
        vscode.commands.registerCommand('markerPerf.publish', () => {
            const editor = vscode.window.activeTextEditor;
            if (!editor) {
                return;
            }

            for (let collectionIndex = 0; collectionIndex < collections.length; collectionIndex++) {
                const diagnostics = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, (_, index) => {
                    const line = (collectionIndex * 1000) + index;
                    return new vscode.Diagnostic(
                        new vscode.Range(line, 0, line, 1),
                        `Marker performance diagnostic ${line + 1}`,
                        vscode.DiagnosticSeverity.Error
                    );
                });

                collections[collectionIndex].set(editor.document.uri, diagnostics);
            }
        }),
        vscode.commands.registerCommand('markerPerf.clear', () => {
            for (const collection of collections) {
                collection.clear();
            }
        })
    );
}

function deactivate() {}

module.exports = { activate, deactivate };
```

## Increasing the number of markers displayed

For this test _maxDiagnosticsPerFile is increased to 6000 so we have more diagnostics to work with. You can also just use the extension example above to display more diagnostics in the output panel. I test the performance with a diagnostic limit of 500 (current), 2000 and 5000 and look at the impact. The results below are in milliseconds and they are taken by looking for the duration of the `_updateDecorations` call on the checker.ts file. The following result are on my normal machine M2 macbook without CPU throttling. 

| Marker decoration limit | `_updateDecorations` run 1 | Run 2 | Run 3 | Run 4 | Run 5 | Median |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 500 |  11.05 | 12.65  | 10.87  | 11.34  | 12.02  |  11.34 |
| 2,000 |  22.52 | 23.68 |  24.08 | 22.03 | 22.13 | 22.52  |
| 5,000 |  28.95 |  29.93 | 30.05 | 27.08 | 29.01 | 29.01  |

Generally it would seem it is safe to increase the marker decoration limit to 5000 if one is using an M2 macbook pro. I will try to do the same test with CPU throttling and analyze the results. I am using the recommended 4x slow down using the Chrome Dev tools. 

| Marker decoration limit | `_updateDecorations` run 1 | Run 2 | Run 3 | Run 4 | Run 5 | Median |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 500 |  21.20 | 19.56  | 15.98  | 23.45  | 22.22  |  21.20 |
| 2,000 |  30.42 | 27.2 |  35.67 | 34.21 | 28.90 | 30.42  |
| 5,000 |  69.48 |  70.01 | 66.93 | 73.34 | 71.09 | 70.01  |

Even when there is 4x CPU throttling, and we are using 5000 markers in the editor, the runs are not very time consuming. Therefore I think it would be safe to increase the limit to 5000.

## Summary

- Detect overflow of displayed markers, and notify consumers of the limit being reached in the language status bar.
- Reuse the existing language-status warning pattern: **Diagnostic highlights — only 5000 shown for performance reasons**.

## Manual verification

- [x] Open a file reporting more than 5000 diagnostics and inspect Editor Language Status beside the language mode in the status bar.
- [x] Reduce the diagnostic count to 5000 or fewer and confirm the warning disappears.
- [x] Switch files or split editors; confirm the warning follows the active editor and its effective visibility options.
